## Proof

**Setup.** Place the circumcircle $(O)$ as the unit circle in the complex plane, so $O$ is the origin and $|a|=|b|=|c|=|p|=|q|=1$. The orthocenter is $h = a+b+c$. Set $\sigma_1 = a+b+c$, $\sigma_2 = ab+bc+ca$, $\sigma_3 = abc$.

**Key collinearity constraint.** Since $P, Q, H$ are collinear and $P, Q$ lie on the unit circle, the line through $P, Q$ has equation $z + pq\,\bar{z} = p+q$. Substituting $h = \sigma_1$ and using $\bar{\sigma_1} = \frac{\sigma_2}{\sigma_3}$:

$$\sigma_1 + pq\cdot\frac{\sigma_2}{\sigma_3} = p+q \implies \sigma_1\sigma_3 + pq\,\sigma_2 = \sigma_3(p+q). \tag{$\star$}$$

**Computing $M$.** The line through $P$ perpendicular to $AP$ has direction $i(p-a)$, so $m = p + t\,i(p-a)$ for real $t$. The line $BC$ (for $b,c$ on the unit circle) has equation $z + bc\,\bar{z} = b+c$. Substituting and solving (using $\bar{p}=1/p$, $\overline{i(p-a)} = -i\cdot\frac{a-p}{ap}$):

$$m = \frac{p\,\sigma_2 - \sigma_3}{ap + bc}.$$

**Computing $\bar{m}$.** Using $\bar{\sigma_2} = \sigma_1/\sigma_3$ and $\bar{\sigma_3} = 1/\sigma_3$:

$$\bar{m} = \frac{\bar{p}\,\bar{\sigma_2} - \bar{\sigma_3}}{\overline{ap+bc}} = \frac{\frac{\sigma_1}{p\sigma_3} - \frac{1}{\sigma_3}}{\frac{ap+bc}{apbc}} = \frac{(\sigma_1 - p)\,abc}{\sigma_3(ap+bc)} = \frac{\sigma_1 - p}{ap+bc}.$$

Therefore:
$$\frac{m}{\bar{m}} = \frac{p\,\sigma_2 - \sigma_3}{\sigma_1 - p}. \tag{1}$$

By symmetry (replacing $p$ with $q$):
$$\frac{n}{\bar{n}} = \frac{q\,\sigma_2 - \sigma_3}{\sigma_1 - q}. \tag{2}$$

**The candidate point.** Consider a point $X$ on the unit circle ($|X|=1$, so $\bar{X}=1/X$) lying on the line through $P$ perpendicular to $OM$. The perpendicularity condition $(X-P)\perp OM$ gives:

$$(X - p)\bar{m} + (\bar{X} - \bar{p})\,m = 0 \implies (X-p)\!\left(\bar{m} - \frac{m}{Xp}\right) = 0.$$

So either $X = p$ or $X = \dfrac{m}{p\,\bar{m}}$. Define $X := \dfrac{m}{p\,\bar{m}}$. Then:

- **$X$ is on the unit circle:** $|X| = \frac{|m|}{|p|\,|\bar{m}|} = \frac{|m|}{|m|} = 1$. ✓
- **$X$ is on the line through $P$ perpendicular to $OM$:** by construction. ✓

**$X$ is also on the line through $Q$ perpendicular to $ON$.** This requires $X = \frac{n}{q\,\bar{n}}$, i.e., $\frac{m/\bar{m}}{n/\bar{n}} = \frac{p}{q}$. Using (1) and (2), this is equivalent to:

$$q\,(p\,\sigma_2 - \sigma_3)(\sigma_1 - q) = p\,(q\,\sigma_2 - \sigma_3)(\sigma_1 - p).$$

Expanding both sides and taking the difference:

$$\text{LHS} - \text{RHS} = (p - q)\big[pq\,\sigma_2 + \sigma_3(\sigma_1 - p - q)\big].$$

By the collinearity constraint $(\star)$: $\sigma_1\sigma_3 + pq\,\sigma_2 = \sigma_3(p+q)$, i.e., $pq\,\sigma_2 + \sigma_3(\sigma_1 - p - q) = 0$.

Therefore $\text{LHS} - \text{RHS} = (p-q)\cdot 0 = 0$. ✓

**Conclusion.** The point $X = \frac{m}{p\,\bar{m}} = \frac{n}{q\,\bar{n}}$ lies on the unit circle $(O)$, on the line through $P$ perpendicular to $OM$, and on the line through $Q$ perpendicular to $ON$. Hence these two lines meet at a point on $(O)$. $\blacksquare$

